= Askew's Race against Time

Askew's days are numbered. The only reason President DuFour is keeping him around is solely by the fact of his foreign policy expertise, and his claim that he's only 'one hundred meters from the finish line' from achieving a huge breakthrough in terms of securing an abolishment of War Loans at Vera and the Commonwealth's rearmament, an issue close to DuFour's heart. The only hope for Askew's political salvation rests in those conferences. [? if not dufour_sacked: There is also no precedent of the President sacking a government that still enjoys a parliamentary majority. ?]

However, General Werner Staal sees Askew as the biggest obstacle in his '<span class="tooltip-text" title="Coined by conservative elites like Staal and Goodryke, this strategy aims to control the dynamic Royalist movement and integrate it into the state by introducing the party to government. In their eyes, this would moderate the Royalists, transforming them into a constructive component of the state.">taming strategy</span>', as Askew has refused to take serious efforts toward bringing the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span> into government. The sense of urgency Staal has invoked onto the issue, along with his convincing of DuFour's State Secretary Oren Marsden and his nephew Symonnet, has brought DuFour to make an early and rash decision on the matter.

- @schleicher_time

@schleicher_time
title: Chancellor Askew is sacked!
subtitle: President DuFour dismisses the government.
go-to: successor_menu

Former Chancellor Askew has been dismissed—despite commanding a parliamentary majority. It appears Staal has struck an unwritten deal with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>, promising “fruitful cooperation” with a new right-wing cabinet that[? if loyalist_nationwide_ban:&nbsp;lifted the ban on the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span> and?] called for new Assembly elections.

It’s a shame that Staal would fall for [+ royalist_leader +]’s lies. But the real question now is: who will succeed Askew?

@successor_menu

- @goerdeler
- @westarp
- @papen

@goerdeler
title: Reynold Goodlake?
subtitle: The Commonwealth Price Commissioner and the Mayor of Lindholt. [? if traditionalist_ideology == "Radical": Former?] <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span> member.
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: goerdeler_seen = 1
face-image: img/portraits/goerdeler.jpg
go-to: successor_menu

To most political observers, Reynold Goodlake, Mayor of Lindholt and the Commonwealth Prize Commissioner, and being a fiscal conservative arguing for a wide reaching reforms of the Commonwealth welfare state and finances, was the expected successor to Askew, with his name even being floated around as the Minister-President of Heartland of a <span style="color: #000000;">Unionists</span>-<span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> coalition.

However, his demands for authorization of sweeping fiscal reforms is too similar to that of Askew in Staal's eyes, who supports a public works solution to the Commonwealth's economic woes. Goodlake will not be the new chancellor.

@westarp
title: Edgar Westmore?
subtitle: DuFour's preferred choice, and [? if traditionalist_leader != "Westmore":former?] <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span> party leader.
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: westarp_seen = 1
face-image: img/portraits/Kuno_von_Westarp.jpg
go-to: successor_menu

DuFour's personal preference is the [? if traditionalist_leader != "Westmore":former?] <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span> leader that played a critical role in securing DuFour's reelection. The two has maintained contact over the past years, and share a hatred of Mallin. Despite this, in a meeting with DuFour, Westmore has declined the chancellorship and argued against dismissing Askew, and against new elections that would destroy the Commonwealth middle parties.

Even when DuFour was reduced to tears, a strategy that had worked on Askew before, Westmore remains firm in his refusal. Westmore will not be the new chancellor.

@papen
title: Ferris Goodryke?
subtitle: An relatively unknown Faithful Conservative from the <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span>. Staal's preferred candidate.
on-arrival: marcher_toleration = 0; marcher_toleration_right = 0; chancellor = "Goodryke"; chancellor_party = "I"; staal_papen_r -= 1
view-if: westarp_seen and goerdeler_seen
face-image: img/portraits/GoodrykeFranz.jpg

With Goodlake and Westmore out of the equation, DuFour has grasped the straw Staal offered and appointed a far-right Faithful Conservative, Ferris Goodryke, from the <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span>, as chancellor. Goodryke is relatively unknown, whose political experience is limited to two terms in the Heartland state Assembly. The two have been friends since their days in the Heartland military academy.

Despite [+ unionist_leader +]'s urging and Goodryke's own promise to refuse, DuFour has used the same tactic on him as he used on Askew, convincing him to accept the position. Due to Goodryke breaking his promise to [+ unionist_leader +] just after he made it, expulsion proceedings against him are interrupted by his resignation from the <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span>, forever ruining his relations with the party necessary for a parliamentary majority as they strongly denounce him as the "great betrayer of the <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span>".

It's unknown of the exact reasons why Staal would choose a man like Goodryke as Askew's successor, but we can assume he was trying to secure the support of the <span style="color: #000000;">Unionist Party</span> and <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalists</span>, as he's a far-right member of the former, and that Goodryke would be an easy person to control.

Time will tell if the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> will honor its promises to Staal, which they'd purposefully refused to write on paper.